{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553143-fan-craft-choshen-shamayim-the-breastplate-of-heaven","id":1553143,"title":"[Fan Craft] Choshen Shamayim - The Breastplate of Heaven","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-08-13T11:30:08-07:00","id":1553143,"title":"[Fan Craft] Choshen Shamayim - The Breastplate of Heaven","content":"<br /> <br /> &quot;Do not wait for death to find the perfect world; clear the dross away from this one instead.&quot;<br /> <br /> The Holy One, Blessed Be He, is always with us, closer than our own hair, yet farther than the farthest star. By learning His Torah, they cling to Him; by applying His Torah, they take the qlipot away and reveal the holiness in all things. Through study, prayer, meditation, and activism, they strive to bring Prophecy (and the values of the Prophets) back to the world. This is the way of the talmidim (students) of the Choshen Shamayim.<br /> <br /> Unlike the Lions of Zion, who trace their ideological roots (especially their limitations on who can study) to the ghettos of pre-modern Lithuania, the Breastplate of Heaven traces its roots to the Safed Circle, a group of Sephardic legalists and mystics who codified both the halakha (the Beit Yosef/Shulchan Arukh by Maran Yosef Karo) and the kabbalah (the Kitvei Ari, by the Ari HaKodesh Rabbi Yitzchak Luria and Rabbi Chayim Vital) after the expulsion of the Sephardic Jews from Iberia. The Safed Circle taught that the study of kabbalah would bring about the Final Redemption, and thus does the Choshen believe. They obtained a renaissance with the coming of Shalom Sharabi (the Rashash) to Jerusalem, and the expansion of Yeshivat HaMekubalim to a network of yeshivot that came with the advent of the Rashash.<br /> <br /> Beyond the rabbis of the Safed Circle and the Rashash, they also claim various Torah giants who have promoted the study of kabbalah throughout the world, such as Rabbi Moshe Chaim Luzzato, the Baal Shem Tov, the Ben Ish Chai, Rav A. I. Kook ZT''L, and Rabbi Aryeh Kaplan ZT''L. In addition to teaching the Zohar and the Kitvei Ari, they also teach the works of these authors, thus coming to view God as a being of infinite unity who interpenetrates all things through the medium of the sefirot. They do not view themselves as Mages, but rather as talmidim and miracle-workers. The group only attempts to actively recruit Jewish men over 20, however, if an Awakened Jewish woman or gentile (so long as they embrace the Noachide Laws) comes to them, they will find a teacher within the Choshen.<br /> <br /> <b>Organization</b>:<br /> <br /> The Choshen operates on a structure of teachers and students, with the highest ranking or most powerful members of the Choshen being students of the Choshen's leader who resides in Jerusalem. Students are expected to both follow and question the instructions of their teachers, and teachers are expected to treat their students with kindness and intellectual respect. Who is whose teacher can be fluid, and one talmid can have more than one teacher at a time. Internal conflicts are often resolved by rigorous intellectual debate. No talmid of the Choshen can be without teacher, and pacts are often made by teachers to take on each other's students if one teacher were to die. <br /> <br /> <b>Affinity Sphere Options</b>: Life, Spirit, or Time. The talmidim of the Choshen are healers, prophets, and those who call down angels.<br /> <br /> <b>Focus</b>:<br /> <br /> Every talmid knows that <i>All Power Comes from God</i>, who reflects His glory through a <i>Creation that is Innately Divine and Alive</i>. More scientifically minded talmidim of the Choshen may note the Sefer Yetzirah insists that <i>Everything is Data</i>, in line with the paradigms of several more scientifically minded organizations.<br /> <br /> The core of a talmid's arts is always either Faith or God-Bonding (both representing the theosophical kabbalah that is the Choshen's primary concern), but many augment that core with High Ritual (representing the practical kabbalah) and sometimes alchemy (since many great kabbalists were also alchemists, such as Rabbi Chayim Vital). The <i>Everything is Data</i> types mentioned above, as well as those who believe generally that the Torah is exalted through the study of knowledge respected by all nations, may integrate technomancy, while martially minded talmidim may integrate Martial Arts as a mystical practice.<br /> <br /> Books, prayers, blessings, and meditation are the core instruments that all talmidim are taught to use. In the case of the practical kabbalah, it is almost entirely books that are accepted as part of Torah by the Ari (such as the Brit Menucha) or scholars after him that are used as guides, although a Hebrew translation of the Solomonic Codes used by the Taftani to bind djinni are also used by the Choshen to bind hostile shedim.<br /> <br /> <b>Stereotypes</b>:<br /> <br /> <b>Lions of Zion</b> - &quot;They are the Litvaks; we are not. Eilu v'eilu devarim elokhim chai, and I will say no more so that I do not speak ill of sages of my tribe.&quot;<br /> <br /> <b>Celestial Chorus</b> - &quot;Though they have failed to spread total belief in divine unity, they are still an important force on the front lines of the Ascension War. We are open to joining them as one of, what do they call them, the Theological Orders?&quot;<br /> <br /> <b>Order of Hermes</b> - &quot;It is a sign of the decay of knowledge that they are called kabbalists while conjuring demons and not praising the LORD. How they expect to understand Solomon's Arts when they do not even value his Proverbs is beyond me.&quot;<br /> <br /> <b>Disparate Alliance</b> - &quot;An interesting prospect. If they approach us to join them against the Nephandi, we might just.&quot;<br /> <br /> <b>Nephandi</b> - &quot;Destructive creatures who worship husks that conceal holiness, rather than striving for holiness themselves. For the sake of saving all of humanity, the Nephandi must be defeated wherever they are found.&quot;<br /> <br /> <b>Technocratic Union</b> - &quot;While they are not themselves the Nephandi, they are the godless ghoul left behind by the corpse of the Order of Reason, controlled by the Nephandi's will. Humanity shall be saved through righteousness and loving-kindness, not Control.&quot;<br /> <br /> [OOC Notes: I'd like feedback.]","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26432-jagnestormskull","name":"JagneStormskull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26432_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-13T17:31:33-07:00","id":1553149,"title":"","content":"They'd have something to say about the Cult of Ecstasy. The tradition you're looking at/ reworking celebrated holy ecstasy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-13T17:45:57-07:00","id":1553151,"title":"","content":"I don\u2019t understand enough about Jewish tradition to understand the difference between them and the Lions of Zion (and in fact am only barely aware of who the Lions of Zion are, except that they are a group of Jewish mages). And I definitely don\u2019t speak Hebrew. Can you explain the differences between the two groups in terms a novice can understand?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-13T21:34:58-07:00","id":1553160,"title":"","content":"The very simple reason is to reflect the division between Ashkenazi Jewish tradition (Central and Eastern European based) and Sephardi Jewish tradition (Western Europe, and parts of North Africa based) that diverged significantly in a number of cultural ways. As written the Chosen Shamayim are more &quot;liberal&quot; (reducing the traditional age of requirement for deep study, being more open to women and non-Jews, etc.) than the Lions of Zion, but that feels like it would be hard to really show in-play since both are still very orthodox coded groups.<br /> <br /> For me, the big problem is that two Jewish Crafts on top of a significant number of Jews in the Celestial Chorus and Hermetics (assuming Jews that don't join other Traditions because they're not driven by faith as a major motivator for said choices) is a lot of Jewish mages. Even more so when you have so many focused on Orthodox Jewish theology and very little focus in the game on other aspects of Jewish philosophy and ethnic traditions.<br /> <br /> It also seems to reduce the Lions of Zion (who aren't specifically Askenaz in their philosophy and that more reflects the authors lack of knowledge about intra-Jewish topics) who were intended to draw from all Rabbinic Jewish groups that formed in response to the Roman expulsion; instead of just the ones that got all the focus in Sorcerer's Crusade. The setup posed in this write-up kinda leaves Jewish mages out of having formed their own Fellowship/Crafts for almost 1,500 years. In the default game take, the Lions of Zion formed much earlier (or at least a predecessor group did) and would have spread throughout Europe and the Levant as Rabbinic Judaism did in reality.<br /> <br /> Ultimately, I think this would work better if you wanted to replace the Lions of Zion because even Brucato acknowledged they weren't part of the setting he thought was a good idea in hindsight, rather than a group meant to be their contemporaries. When it feels like you're going to end up making other groups less compelling by taking aspects out of them to make room for your idea, it's worth considering if there's really enough room in the game-as-is for your idea.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T10:01:18-07:00","id":1553173,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amethyst</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553143-fan-craft-choshen-shamayim-the-breastplate-of-heaven?p=1553151#post1553151\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don\u2019t understand enough about Jewish tradition to understand the difference between them and the Lions of Zion (and in fact am only barely aware of who the Lions of Zion are, except that they are a group of Jewish mages). And I definitely don\u2019t speak Hebrew. Can you explain the differences between the two groups in terms a novice can understand?</div> </div> </div> </div>Heavy Arms' first paragraph basically summed it up. The difference is that the Choshen are relatively more open than the Lions. Both groups would likely affirm many of the same concepts, but differ over interpretations. The Lions are a bit of a stereotype (as many things are from nineties Mage), but I was hesitant about a complete rewrite since I didn't want to violate existing canon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26432-jagnestormskull","name":"JagneStormskull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26432_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T10:37:48-07:00","id":1553175,"title":"","content":"Possibly it would work better if the Shamayim and the LoZ were not separate entities but factions within the larger body of Jewish mages. When the plausible number of members gets to small it becomes hard to explain the existence of separate organizations, but it is considerably easier to consider traditions and schools of thought.<br /> <br /> On another note, because of how the Celestial Chorus is often written, I have profound skepticism that any mages orthodox in their faith would reasonably join with them, but I don't know enough about either the Ashkenazi or Sephardi to say for sure.. Of course it depends a great deal on how much you show up the Chorus' heterodox and Theosophic side and how much you pave over that and treat them like an ecumenical alliance of necessity. The first paragraph of their writeup in M20 takes pains to show how they defy orthodoxy, though.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T10:51:35-07:00","id":1553176,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553143-fan-craft-choshen-shamayim-the-breastplate-of-heaven?p=1553160#post1553160\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The very simple reason is to reflect the division between Ashkenazi Jewish tradition (Central and Eastern European based) and Sephardi Jewish tradition (Western Europe, and parts of North Africa based) that diverged significantly in a number of cultural ways. As written the Chosen Shamayim are more &quot;liberal&quot; (<b>reducing the traditional age of requirement for deep study</b>, being more open to women and non-Jews, etc.) than the Lions of Zion, but that feels like it would be hard to really show in-play since both are still very orthodox coded groups.</div> </div> </div> </div>I bolded this because I'd like to provide a note. The Choshen does not reduce the traditional age of requirement for deep study; they never had that tradition (and view that tradition as reactionary to say the least). There's a difference. The actual traditional age of kabbalah study according to the Safed Circle is 20, not 40. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For me, the big problem is that two Jewish Crafts on top of a significant number of Jews in the Celestial Chorus and Hermetics (assuming Jews that don't join other Traditions because they're not driven by faith as a major motivator for said choices) is a lot of Jewish mages. Even more so when you have so many focused on Orthodox Jewish theology and very little focus in the game on other aspects of Jewish philosophy and ethnic traditions.<br /> <br /> It also seems to reduce the Lions of Zion (who aren't specifically Askenaz in their philosophy and that more reflects the authors lack of knowledge about intra-Jewish topics) who were intended to draw from all Rabbinic Jewish groups that formed in response to the Roman expulsion; instead of just the ones that got all the focus in Sorcerer's Crusade. The setup posed in this write-up kinda leaves Jewish mages out of having formed their own Fellowship/Crafts for almost 1,500 years. In the default game take, the Lions of Zion formed much earlier (or at least a predecessor group did) and would have spread throughout Europe and the Levant as Rabbinic Judaism did in reality.<br /> <br /> Ultimately, I think this would work better if you wanted to replace the Lions of Zion because even Brucato acknowledged they weren't part of the setting he thought was a good idea in hindsight, rather than a group meant to be their contemporaries. When it feels like you're going to end up making other groups less compelling by taking aspects out of them to make room for your idea, it's worth considering if there's really enough room in the game-as-is for your idea. </div> </div> </div>I suppose that all makes sense. I was hesitant about a full-on rewrite because I didn't want to violate the canon, but knowing that Brucato has admitted that he regrets it, I could do it as a complete rewrite.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26432-jagnestormskull","name":"JagneStormskull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26432_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T11:05:19-07:00","id":1553178,"title":"","content":"There's actually a very simple draw the Chorus has: people who value Orthodox Jewish theology but not the patriarchy that has infested it.<br /> <br /> Like, there is no theological basis for there being no women rabbis. The rabbinical traditions against it are pretty flimsy; and at this point attempts to change this are met with pretty ugly chauvinism over any real theological debates (a NYC Jewish seminary started ordaining orthodox women rabbis and it went very shittily). The Chorus isn't really going to be concerned with such internal religious struggles, so if you want egalitarian orthodox Judaism, it's a lot easier to join the Chorus than change the minds of the Lions. <br /> <br /> This is why my &quot;too many Jewish mages&quot; is a bit more nuanced than just the raw numbers, but also includes some narrative aspects as well. Jewish mages that value their faith have some serious choices about what factions to join. Unless they were raised by mages/acolytes to be predisposed to their fictional factional idiosyncrasies, none of the groups are a pure 100% fit. The more groups you have (even if you disregard the issues with how to populate those groups) the less tension there is in joining the faction that feels close enough to what you want instead of being able to shop around for a good fit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T11:12:36-07:00","id":1553179,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JagneStormskull</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553143-fan-craft-choshen-shamayim-the-breastplate-of-heaven?p=1553176#post1553176\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I bolded this because I'd like to provide a note. The Choshen does not reduce the traditional age of requirement for deep study; they never had that tradition (and view that tradition as reactionary to say the least). There's a difference. The actual traditional age of kabbalah study according to the Safed Circle is 20, not 40.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't want to get into a deep theological history debate on this, but binah studies being something that was traditionally for older men (40 has a long history in Jewish theological writings as a placeholder number for &quot;old&quot; rather than any specific attachment to the age of 40) is a few centuries older than the Safed Circle was. The Ashkenazi rabbinical tendencies to never find a fence they didn't think needed another fence around it is similarly more recent, but both groups were reacting to a much older rabbinical tradition.<br /> <br /> The Safed Circle also just didn't consider kabbalism to be binah as a reason to reduce when to start integrating it into religious study. most of them didn't reject the concept that binah was for older students.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I suppose that all makes sense. I was hesitant about a full-on rewrite because I didn't want to violate the canon, but knowing that Brucato has admitted that he regrets it, I could do it as a complete rewrite. </div> </div> </div>Regret... isn't the word I would personally choose to describe his comments on that, but I really don't think re litigating that mess is useful. The important part is, as you noted, a rewrite is amply justifiable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T12:12:20-07:00","id":1553180,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/\">Ramnesis</a><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> On another note, because of how the Celestial Chorus is often written, I have profound skepticism that any mages orthodox in their faith would reasonably join with them, but I don't know enough about either the Ashkenazi or Sephardi to say for sure.. Of course it depends a great deal on how much you show up the Chorus' heterodox and Theosophic side and how much you pave over that and treat them like an ecumenical alliance of necessity. The first paragraph of their writeup in M20 takes pains to show how they defy orthodoxy, though. </div> </div> </div>Frankly, I don't think being a mage is really compatible with remaining an orthodox believer in basically any religion. Like, kinda by definition you're going to run into other mages who don't follow your religion, at which point you have a real problem. Like, to use an example alluded to in the write-up above, what happens when a talmid meets a Hermetic who can throw lightning with the power of bad Hebrew? Oh, and they also have a made up angel language that you've never heard of that still works...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T12:49:20-07:00","id":1553181,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553143-fan-craft-choshen-shamayim-the-breastplate-of-heaven?p=1553175#post1553175\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On another note, because of how the Celestial Chorus is often written, I have profound skepticism that any mages orthodox in their faith would reasonably join with them, but I don't know enough about either the Ashkenazi or Sephardi to say for sure.. Of course it depends a great deal on how much you show up the Chorus' heterodox and Theosophic side and how much you pave over that and treat them like an ecumenical alliance of necessity. The first paragraph of their writeup in M20 takes pains to show how they defy orthodoxy, though.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know that they're &quot;often&quot; written as specifically being theosophic. For one thing, the Celestial Chorus has been letting in members of religions besides Christianity for longer than theosophy has existed. I also don't know how the diversity of thought we see in the Celestial Chorus Tradition Book is specifically theosophic. If the Chorus only follows the broad ideas of theosophy (not the specific movement), such as emanationism from the One, and reincarnation, then the kabbalah supports both.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26432-jagnestormskull","name":"JagneStormskull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26432_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T13:02:47-07:00","id":1553182,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553143-fan-craft-choshen-shamayim-the-breastplate-of-heaven?p=1553180#post1553180\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/\">Ramnesis</a><br /> <br /> <br /> Frankly, I don't think being a mage is really compatible with remaining an orthodox believer in basically any religion. Like, kinda by definition you're going to run into other mages who don't follow your religion, at which point you have a real problem. Like, to use an example alluded to in the write-up above, what happens when a talmid meets a Hermetic who can throw lightning with the power of bad Hebrew? Oh, and they also have a made up angel language that you've never heard of that still works...</div> </div> </div> </div>By that logic, Mages shouldn't be able to have True Faith... yet it's in the corebook. And nothing says that your True Faith will immediately be broken upon seeing another Mage cast a spell. As for what happens, the talmid doesn't think it's impossible... just that it's irresponsible at best, and dark at worst. Hierarchical paradigm, not closed paradigm.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26432-jagnestormskull","name":"JagneStormskull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26432_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T13:11:01-07:00","id":1553183,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553143-fan-craft-choshen-shamayim-the-breastplate-of-heaven?p=1553149#post1553149\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They'd have something to say about the Cult of Ecstasy. The tradition you're looking at/ reworking celebrated holy ecstasy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm... that's an interesting prospect. At first, my thoughts on most of the Traditions besides the Celestial Chorus and the Order of Hermes were &quot;well, they aren't infringing on our thing&quot; (which, for the record, is why they're open to joining the Disparate Alliance, &quot;they're doing their thing, we're doing ours&quot;), but you're right. Mystical experiences and altered states of consciousness are a big thing in kabbalah... Breslover Hasids even smoke more weed per capita than non-Orthodox Jews. Mentally, I sort the Cult of Ecstasy into the &quot;Indian Traditions&quot; category because of the whole &quot;Sahajiya&quot; thing, but there is a diversity of thought within the Cult of Ecstasy... I'll think about it more on the next update.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26432-jagnestormskull","name":"JagneStormskull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26432_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T15:45:02-07:00","id":1553188,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JagneStormskull</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553143-fan-craft-choshen-shamayim-the-breastplate-of-heaven?p=1553182#post1553182\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">By that logic, Mages shouldn't be able to have True Faith... yet it's in the corebook.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean, lots of stuff is in the core book. It's not the most consistent of games. See for example <i>How do you DO that?</i><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for what happens, the talmid doesn't think it's impossible... just that it's irresponsible at best, and dark at worst. Hierarchical paradigm, not closed paradigm. </div> </div> </div>Precisely. Your talmid is already having to actively invent excuses to explain other mages' magic. If that's not exactly outside of orthodoxy, it's certainly outside of normal conceptions of religiosity. Like, I really doubt many people's conceptions of their Judaism encompasses an explanation for how John Dee-style Enochian both works and is irresponsible.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T16:40:14-07:00","id":1553189,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553143-fan-craft-choshen-shamayim-the-breastplate-of-heaven?p=1553188#post1553188\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Precisely. Your talmid is already having to actively invent excuses to explain other mages' magic.</div> </div> </div> </div>Isn't that something all mages need to do? Hermetics need to make an excuse for why Virtual Adepts can hack reality, and the Iterator needs to make an excuse for why that Dreamspeaker turned a girder into a walking steel monster by talking to it. More importantly, the Iterator needs to figure out why their specialists in the same thing (Void Engineers) can't do the same thing and get the same result.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Like, I really doubt many people's conceptions of their Judaism encompasses an explanation for how John Dee-style Enochian both works and is irresponsible. </div> </div> </div>Most people's conception of their Judaism doesn't encounter the setting of MtAs. That said, I could come up with an in-character explanation for why it's both works and is irresponsible - it might be a real discovered language of spirits, but those spirits aren't necessarily angels as Dee believed, evidenced by the whole wife-sharing thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26432,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26432-jagnestormskull","user":"JagneStormskull","date":". 08-15-2025, 12:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26432-jagnestormskull","name":"JagneStormskull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26432_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T20:36:03-07:00","id":1553193,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553143-fan-craft-choshen-shamayim-the-breastplate-of-heaven?p=1553180#post1553180\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/\">Ramnesis</a><br /> <br /> <br /> Frankly, I don't think being a mage is really compatible with remaining an orthodox believer in basically any religion.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's somewhat true, but not because of any of those things.<br /> <br /> First, while Mages frequently encounter phenomena that goes against orthodox belief, they can explain those phenomena away however they want. What my fellow Mage says about their Magic has no real bearing on what I believe about their Magic. Just as IRL religious fundamentalists are perfectly able to keep their beliefs while inhabiting a world where so much information and technology is predicated on scientific truths they don't believe in, Mages can completely ignore the paradigms of other Mages and either explain their powers in some other way, or not really care enough, without having their faith blown up.<br /> <br /> Second, orthodoxy itself means something else for Mages. In fact, it already means something other than what you think for many theologians. It isn't necessarily about setting in stone specific or interpretations, literal or not, it is about holding to particular <i>traditions</i> and worldviews. Accepting any particular phenomena isn't necessarily hurting orthodoxy, because that's not the point (although it may hurt a given orthodox thinker's <i>faith</i>, but that's completely on the person, not on the orthodoxy). Orthodox theologians debating the meaning of a given information within their worldview isn't just something that happens, it is common in every religion.<br /> <br /> A Mage that adheres to an orthodox worldview and tradition is then perfectly normal to begin with. But Mages go a step further, because by the very nature of being a Mage their powers reinforce and prove their beliefs, and their belief in the &quot;righteousness&quot;, so to speak, of their world view is more important than it being objectively correct. For a Mage it isn't so much about proving that the world works the way you think it works, it's about fighting to <b>make</b> it work that way, and to stop <b>real threats</b> on how the world works. At the end of the day, being a Mage is about moving past the stance of imposing your worldview upon others, into imposing your worldview upon reality itself. It's within this framework that an orthodox Mage is orthodox.<br /> <br /> What really makes it hard for a Mage to abide to orthodoxy <b>completely</b> isn't any external threat to this orthodoxy, but the fact that being a Mage also means being defiant. Any person that simply accept reality as it is (or as they think it is), simply isn't a Mage. They're Willworkers by definition, imposing their own personal views, not those of others. Even in the Technocracy it's a fact, even if an unpleasant one, that truly beating Agents into conformity doesn't work. Any Mage is, at the end of the day, someone who defies, explores and creates, and that can't really, fundamentally, accept anyone's else imposition on their beliefs. They <b>do</b> the imposing, with prejudice.<br /> <br /> But then, that only means we have to understand orthodoxy under a very different light when it comes to Mages, no matter which group they belong to or which belief system they ascribe to. But that doesn't means there's no merit on talking about orthodoxy, so it means we have to expand the concept for it to make sense in their context.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T22:42:11-07:00","id":1553197,"title":"","content":"There's also an additional aspect where &quot;orthodox&quot; has a very specific meaning in Jewish theology that is separate from larger discourse about orthodoxy (religious or otherwise).<br /> <br /> Judaism is actually a pretty easy religion for being a mage because Judaism doesn't claim exclusive access to things like mystical powers or communion with spiritual entities. You can sum up a huge amount of Jewish theological writings as basically, &quot;here's why our way is better than other peoples' ways of doing things.&quot; Which is already a starting position that other people can do stuff without requiring it to be reinterpreted. There's a very famous part of the Biblical story where Moses has a miracle show-down with some Egyptian priests. The Egyptians perform a miracle, and Moses one-ups them. This isn't presented as because the Egyptians are charlatans or worshipers of false idols, but because the Lord is the real supreme deity and the Egyptian pantheon are something lesser; hence Moses gets superior miracle powers. There's no need for a rationalization of why Egyptian priests can do some miracles despite not worshiping the Lord. When it's a given in Jewish metaphysical thinking that Jews don't have a lock on metaphysical power by virtue of worshiping the correct deity... adapting to the realities of the Mage setting isn't such a hard leap.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-15T08:59:35-07:00","id":1553202,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JagneStormskull</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553143-fan-craft-choshen-shamayim-the-breastplate-of-heaven?p=1553181#post1553181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't know that they're &quot;often&quot; written as specifically being theosophic. For one thing, the Celestial Chorus has been letting in members of religions besides Christianity for longer than theosophy has existed. I also don't know how the diversity of thought we see in the Celestial Chorus Tradition Book is specifically theosophic. If the Chorus only follows the broad ideas of theosophy (not the specific movement), such as emanationism from the One, and reincarnation, then the kabbalah supports both.</div> </div> </div> </div>There are several of the Traditions that are basically 19th and 20th century occult organizations with the serial numbers filed off and backdated several thousand years. The Chorus and Akashics are that for Theosophy (it's even in the Akashic's name). It doesn't always get played up, but it's there.<br /> <br /> On the more general heterodoxical side, the Chorus is quite overt about that to the point that M20 actually opens with the word 'heretics.' I suspect that the CC has its own share of the aforementioned misguided kabbalists. Certainly nothing about the Tradition indicates it would try to stop that from happening.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-15T09:20:46-07:00","id":1553203,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553143-fan-craft-choshen-shamayim-the-breastplate-of-heaven?p=1553197#post1553197\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's also an additional aspect where &quot;orthodox&quot; has a very specific meaning in Jewish theology that is separate from larger discourse about orthodoxy (religious or otherwise).<br /> <br /> Judaism is actually a pretty easy religion for being a mage because Judaism doesn't claim exclusive access to things like mystical powers or communion with spiritual entities. You can sum up a huge amount of Jewish theological writings as basically, &quot;here's why our way is better than other peoples' ways of doing things.&quot; Which is already a starting position that other people can do stuff without requiring it to be reinterpreted. There's a very famous part of the Biblical story where Moses has a miracle show-down with some Egyptian priests. The Egyptians perform a miracle, and Moses one-ups them. This isn't presented as because the Egyptians are charlatans or worshipers of false idols, but because the Lord is the real supreme deity and the Egyptian pantheon are something lesser; hence Moses gets superior miracle powers. There's no need for a rationalization of why Egyptian priests can do some miracles despite not worshiping the Lord. When it's a given in Jewish metaphysical thinking that Jews don't have a lock on metaphysical power by virtue of worshiping the correct deity... adapting to the realities of the Mage setting isn't such a hard leap.</div> </div> </div> </div>Same for Christianity (depending on denomination). We don't necessarily claim that the Egyptian priests couldn't perform miracles, though we take a strong stance on the problems with the entities they were working with. Nor is that an isolated incident. Watch an interview with an exorcist and it is clear they don't think nothing else has power, just that the power pales in comparison to that of God.<br /> <br /> Really I don't think this is a problem for most major religions, which is why I don't think that being a mage is inherently damaging to faith. Some may have more problems dealing with the metaphysics than others, of course, but really all real world religions already exist in a world that doesn't always play along.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-15T09:49:27-07:00","id":1553204,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553143-fan-craft-choshen-shamayim-the-breastplate-of-heaven?p=1553197#post1553197\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's also an additional aspect where &quot;orthodox&quot; has a very specific meaning in Jewish theology that is separate from larger discourse about orthodoxy (religious or otherwise).</div> </div> </div> </div>Right. Being an &quot;Orthodox&quot; Jew is actually more about being &quot;orthoprax&quot; (right practice) than &quot;orthodox&quot; (right belief). &quot;Orthodox Jew&quot; was originally an exonym, and isn't even really a phrase in Hebrew AFAIK. There is &quot;Dati&quot; (religious), but that's a relatively new phrase AFAIK.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Judaism is actually a pretty easy religion for being a mage because Judaism doesn't claim exclusive access to things like mystical powers or communion with spiritual entities. You can sum up a huge amount of Jewish theological writings as basically, &quot;here's why our way is better than other peoples' ways of doing things.&quot; Which is already a starting position that other people can do stuff without requiring it to be reinterpreted. There's a very famous part of the Biblical story where Moses has a miracle show-down with some Egyptian priests. The Egyptians perform a miracle, and Moses one-ups them. This isn't presented as because the Egyptians are charlatans or worshipers of false idols, but because the Lord is the real supreme deity and the Egyptian pantheon are something lesser; hence Moses gets superior miracle powers. There's no need for a rationalization of why Egyptian priests can do some miracles despite not worshiping the Lord. When it's a given in Jewish metaphysical thinking that Jews don't have a lock on metaphysical power by virtue of worshiping the correct deity... adapting to the realities of the Mage setting isn't such a hard leap. </div> </div> </div>Yep. And throughout the Plagues, the Egyptian priests/magicians continue to do miracles/spells... but they can only make things worse, not repair the damage. The Plagues are also themed around showing that LORD controls the domains of the Egyptian deities, hence the statement &quot;I have smote their gods.&quot; Elijah pulls the same thing with the prophets of Baal in the Book of Kings IIRC.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26432,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26432-jagnestormskull","user":"JagneStormskull","date":". 08-15-2025, 12:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26432-jagnestormskull","name":"JagneStormskull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26432_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-15T11:56:16-07:00","id":1553207,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553143-fan-craft-choshen-shamayim-the-breastplate-of-heaven?p=1553202#post1553202\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> There are several of the Traditions that are basically 19th and 20th century occult organizations with the serial numbers filed off and backdated several thousand years.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think all of them are that to some degree, most to a very large one. The CC is definitely Theosophy, just as the OoH is Telema.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553143-fan-craft-choshen-shamayim-the-breastplate-of-heaven?p=1553203#post1553203\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Same for Christianity (depending on denomination).</div> </div> </div> </div>Not in the same sense <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/\">Heavy Arms</a> said, when speaking of the specifics of Judaism.<br /> <br /> For most Christian denominations, the orthodoxy is that <b>if</b> demons and other spirits exist, they're almost powerless. They <b>may</b> be, sometimes, able to do possessions and cause symptoms through that, but that's about it and even that isn't common. Outside Charismatic Christianity the belief in witchcraft, profane (as in non-sacred) magic and regular possessions isn't really orthodox, although it has always been common among the common people. Within the Roman Church it is accepted, but's still fringe, and very few if any Exorcists are really deemed orthodox. Most other denominations follow something similar.<br /> <br /> The thing with Judaism is that their texts don't ever claim YHWH (it's a matter of respect to read this as Adonai or some other name, not as written) to be the sole god in existence, they don't even claim other gods to be anything but true, actual, gods, if lesser ones. In fact, in many texts YHWH doesn't even works alone, although certainly works from a position of leadership. Now, it doesn't mean a &quot;strictly monotheistic&quot; <b>belief</b> isn't possible within Judaism, or even uncommon, nor that you can't interpret passages in a way to see demons in those gods. It's just that this is on you, not on the text, and it doesn't affect Orthodox views. YHWH is the God of the Hebrews/Jews, and the superior god, but other gods for other cultures exist and that's perfectly fine.<br /> <br /> It doesn't just means Jewish Mages can have Orthodox beliefs while still accepting that other magics exist. More on point and distinct from Christianity, it means Orthodox Jewish Mages can accept others' Magic <b>at face value</b>, at least to a far greater extent than other religious Mages. They can accept the explanation other Mage gives, even if a religious one, at least as possible. Now, this is also common on &quot;polytheistic&quot; religions, but the details depend on each case and is beyond this discussion.\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-15T12:08:53-07:00","id":1553208,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553143-fan-craft-choshen-shamayim-the-breastplate-of-heaven?p=1553203#post1553203\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Same for Christianity (depending on denomination). We don't necessarily claim that the Egyptian priests couldn't perform miracles, though we take a strong stance on the problems with the entities they were working with. Nor is that an isolated incident. Watch an interview with an exorcist and it is clear they don't think nothing else has power, just that the power pales in comparison to that of God.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem for both Christianity and Islam (which covers a lot of religious people on the planet), is that both embraced some level of evangelism/proselytizing as a matter of doctrine. While both religions accept the Exodus story and thus have to acknowledge that there isn't the demonization of some non-Abrahamic practices (but is of others), other narratives need to come in to justify the need to actively convert people. Both also came into being after Judaism had moved from a more henoistic/soft-monotheism to a hard monotheism, and thus started with more distance from the earlier less exclusive roots of the shared religious background.<br /> <br /> While I'm painting with a very broad brush here, it's worth remembering that a lot of the more modern accepting attitudes in most Abrahamic faith practices is a product of a post-Enlightenment need to stay relevant to shifting broader social norms than a deep set religious push towards such a position. <br /> <br /> The problem for a Christian mage, is that their belief system includes (whether they value it strongly or not) that spreading Christianity to non-Christians is a fundamental good. The Chorus's status as heretical is most strongly found in its inclusivity. &quot;Your job is to support everyone on their journey to finding divine enlightenment through the One,&quot; is at odds with, &quot;Jesus is the only path that gets you there.&quot; Judaism doesn't hold as a fundamental doctrine that you need to actively worship the Lord to be a righteous person that will find redemption and spiritual fulfillment, so there isn't the contradiction. Again, lots of Christians do not personally care about converting non-believers directly, but one has to wonder how many of them are going to actively combine Christianity and magic as a mage if presented with that decision vs. taking a more &quot;heretical&quot; stance on their relationship with divine duty.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JagneStormskull</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1553143-fan-craft-choshen-shamayim-the-breastplate-of-heaven?p=1553204#post1553204\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Right. Being an &quot;Orthodox&quot; Jew is actually more about being &quot;orthoprax&quot; (right practice) than &quot;orthodox&quot; (right belief). &quot;Orthodox Jew&quot; was originally an exonym, and isn't even really a phrase in Hebrew AFAIK. There is &quot;Dati&quot; (religious), but that's a relatively new phrase AFAIK.</div> </div> </div> </div>Haredi (which is an endonym, even if a recent one) is probably the best term for both orthodox and orthoprax, where as Haskalah movements that don't reject orthoprax teachings (aka mostly the Modern Orthodox, and some very traditional Conservative practices that would find themselves pushed out of mainstream Conservative rabbinical teachings) would cover the two major groups of Orthodox Jews using Hebrew language and terms of choice. Though very few people would actively identify as Haskalah any more, since Haskalah as a movement basically succeeded and doesn't require active pursuit anymore; it's just the origin point for most non-Haredi dati traditions in modern times.<br /> <br /> For anyone not familiar:<br /> <br /> Haredi translates as &quot;shaker&quot; or &quot;quaker&quot; which is no coincidence because the Christian movements that use the translated names also derive the name from the book of Isiah where being in awe of the divine caused people to hared or &quot;tremble.&quot; This is interpreted as an ecstatic state, not a negative one, and is seen as requiring strict religious life-styles to achieve.<br /> <br /> Haskalah translates to &quot;wisdom&quot; but more in context, &quot;enlightenment,&quot; and was a set of Jewish movements to go in the opposite direction and balance Judaism with a more modern assimilated life when post-Enlightenment social norms started taking root. With the Enlightenment leading to broad acceptance of religious tolerance as a social good within cultures that integrated the era's ideals, the idea of needing to live separately to maintain personal beliefs weakened (though ideals vs. reality did end up spiking things in Haredi favor for quite some time).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yep. And throughout the Plagues, the Egyptian priests/magicians continue to do miracles/spells... but they can only make things worse, not repair the damage. The Plagues are also themed around showing that LORD controls the domains of the Egyptian deities, hence the statement &quot;I have smote their gods.&quot; Elijah pulls the same thing with the prophets of Baal in the Book of Kings IIRC. </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure there's a single &quot;correct&quot; to recall here. Though I feel that the more intuitive interpretation of Pharaoh's disappointment with his court magicians/etc. stems more from them trying to one-up Moses and Aaron in causing destruction and still ultimately proving less powerful. Given how much of that part of the Exodus story revolves around &quot;softening&quot; Pharoh's heart until he relents and lets Moses lead the Hebrews out of Egypt, whether or not the Egyptians could have done powerful &quot;healing&quot; miracles to counter the destructive plagues isn't really addressed over their desire to try to prove more powerful... the Pharaoh's country getting wrecked in the process be damned. It's a bit easier to see why the Pharaoh has a change a mind when his side is both losing, and it's costing him dearly in practical matters of governing beyond losing face.<br /> <br /> Trying not to get too tangential here, a lot of archeology and history shows that these sorts of &quot;miracle showdowns,&quot; were at least culturally normal (even if they seem to be told in stories rather than witnessed events) for that part of the world. It wasn't really some unique event, or something that would have been considered explicitly &quot;religious&quot; at the time; but rather more of a political gesture between peoples that all assume their deities are real and part of their politics. &quot;The gods don't have our backs on this one, so I'm not going to start a war with those people for that thing they did,&quot; is a potent tool for state-craft that doesn't force either party to reject their peoples' belief systems even if they didn't come out on top of this particular divine display of power.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}